WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.938
- My goal is to simplify complexity.

00:00:03.938 --> 00:00:05.260
You have those, like, shower moments,

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.650
where you're walking at midnight

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.049
in some town in New York City

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:10.358
and you've got these amazing brand ideas,

00:00:10.358 --> 00:00:13.471
and then you started thinking, well,

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:16.638
I could really start
doing this, if only X,

00:00:16.638 --> 00:00:19.708
and if I had this person, or
if this technology existed.

00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:21.241
I think leaders of companies

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:23.609
have to consider themselves editors,

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:26.050
and you constantly have to edit the team.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.960
- He's an American computer programmer

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.009
and entrepreneur.

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:32.119
He's widely known as the
co-founder and CEO of Twitter,

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.589
as well as the founder and CEO of Square.

00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:38.429
He has an estimated net
worth of 1.2 billion dollars.

00:00:38.429 --> 00:00:40.308
He's Jack Dorsey, and here's my take

00:00:40.308 --> 00:00:42.260
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.948
Rule #1 is my personal favorite,

00:00:43.948 --> 00:00:45.790
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.491
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:47.491 --> 00:00:48.919
And as always, if Jack says something

00:00:48.919 --> 00:00:50.428
that really resonates with you,

00:00:50.428 --> 00:00:51.580
make sure to leave it in the comments

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.998
and put quotes around it
so other people can benefit

00:00:53.998 --> 00:00:56.081
and be inspired, as well.

00:01:09.088 --> 00:01:11.050
- It's kind of cliche, but, just do it.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.772
A lot of time is spent
thinking about something

00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:15.605
And talking about it.

00:01:15.605 --> 00:01:16.468
- Yeah.

00:01:16.468 --> 00:01:18.992
- But when you actually
get something down,

00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:21.363
either on paper or in code, or...

00:01:21.363 --> 00:01:24.553
You know, in front of someone.

00:01:24.553 --> 00:01:26.611
That's when things start happening.

00:01:26.611 --> 00:01:30.976
And you're going to
make a lot of mistakes.

00:01:30.976 --> 00:01:32.679
The important thing is
that you make decisions

00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:34.430
and you keep moving forward.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.862
But you have to get it out there.

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:37.695
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:01:37.695 --> 00:01:40.330
- You have to start, and you have to be

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.549
brave enough to take the criticism.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:47.261
I always go back to the
punk movement from the 70s.

00:01:47.261 --> 00:01:49.552
People would get out,
they'd be on the street,

00:01:49.552 --> 00:01:51.081
They'd start these bands.
- 'Cause you were a teenager.

00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:51.914
- Yeah.

00:01:53.539 --> 00:01:55.725
And they would be,

00:01:55.725 --> 00:01:58.674
they would be learning in front of people.

00:01:58.674 --> 00:01:59.794
They get out on the street,

00:01:59.794 --> 00:02:02.014
and it's their first time with a guitar.

00:02:02.014 --> 00:02:03.895
And, you know, they're
learning how to play

00:02:03.895 --> 00:02:05.245
and they're getting better
and better and better.

00:02:05.245 --> 00:02:06.446
- Yeah.

00:02:06.446 --> 00:02:07.465
- And I think it's really important

00:02:07.465 --> 00:02:09.075
to just get it down.

00:02:09.075 --> 00:02:09.926
Do it.

00:02:09.926 --> 00:02:10.759
- [Interviewer] Just do it.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.732
- Edit, edit, edit, edit.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.565
My goal is to simplify complexity.

00:02:16.425 --> 00:02:19.385
I just want to build stuff
that really simplifies

00:02:19.385 --> 00:02:21.304
our base human interaction.

00:02:21.304 --> 00:02:23.523
Twitter was around communication,

00:02:23.523 --> 00:02:26.231
and visualizing what was
happening in the world

00:02:26.231 --> 00:02:27.064
in real time.

00:02:27.064 --> 00:02:29.504
Square was allowing everyone to accept

00:02:29.504 --> 00:02:31.325
the form of payment that people have

00:02:31.325 --> 00:02:33.444
in their pocket today,
which is a credit card.

00:02:33.444 --> 00:02:37.514
So we've built the simplest
way to accept credit cards.

00:02:37.514 --> 00:02:40.033
It's just this little, tiny device

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:41.673
that we give away for free,

00:02:41.673 --> 00:02:44.154
and you just download some
software from the app store,

00:02:44.154 --> 00:02:46.854
and you plug it into your
mobile phone or your iPad,

00:02:46.854 --> 00:02:48.993
and suddenly you can take credit cards.

00:02:48.993 --> 00:02:51.362
So if you're a tax
accountant, or a lawyer,

00:02:51.362 --> 00:02:54.673
or a doctor, or, you
know, even a hairstylist,

00:02:54.673 --> 00:02:56.865
you can now accept credit cards.

00:02:56.865 --> 00:03:01.035
It's really that the users
help decide where we go.

00:03:01.035 --> 00:03:03.743
We are the curators of the technology,

00:03:03.743 --> 00:03:07.594
and we think we're pretty good at it, but,

00:03:07.594 --> 00:03:10.196
and this leads into my third lesson, is,

00:03:10.196 --> 00:03:11.074
you need to listen.

00:03:11.074 --> 00:03:15.039
You need to be still, and really listen to

00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:17.431
what's going on around you.

00:03:17.431 --> 00:03:19.233
And let that inform,

00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:22.971
and let that inspire your next steps.

00:03:22.971 --> 00:03:26.391
- Seems hard to be still and listen

00:03:26.391 --> 00:03:28.492
when you've got your,
you have 300 follower,

00:03:28.492 --> 00:03:30.782
you're following 300 people or whatever.

00:03:30.782 --> 00:03:31.674
- It's extremely hard.

00:03:31.674 --> 00:03:33.637
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:33.637 --> 00:03:35.024
- It's really important

00:03:35.024 --> 00:03:37.774
that you're able to step back and

00:03:39.097 --> 00:03:40.586
listen to what's going on

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:42.255
and what patterns emerge,

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:43.604
so that you can really consider those

00:03:43.604 --> 00:03:44.771
and move them.

00:03:45.706 --> 00:03:47.857
I think that's one of the strongest things

00:03:47.857 --> 00:03:49.482
you can cultivate as an entrepreneur,

00:03:49.482 --> 00:03:51.482
is to, not rely on luck,

00:03:52.975 --> 00:03:56.385
but cultivating an ability to recognize

00:03:56.385 --> 00:03:59.485
fortunate situations
when they're occurring.

00:03:59.485 --> 00:04:01.546
Odeo was a very fortunate situation.

00:04:01.546 --> 00:04:03.283
Could've taken that idea,

00:04:03.283 --> 00:04:07.035
not told it to Ev, not told
it to Biz, not told it to Noah

00:04:07.035 --> 00:04:09.464
and all my other coworkers at Odeo,

00:04:09.464 --> 00:04:12.342
and tried to start my own
company with that idea.

00:04:12.342 --> 00:04:13.843
But the fortunate situation was that

00:04:13.843 --> 00:04:16.361
I was working with some
really amazing people.

00:04:16.361 --> 00:04:19.052
And we had a great team.

00:04:19.052 --> 00:04:22.768
So, of course, you know, it
makes sense to start there.

00:04:22.768 --> 00:04:26.452
The fortunate situation
for Square was that

00:04:26.452 --> 00:04:28.733
in 2008, the end of 2008,

00:04:28.733 --> 00:04:30.704
the entire market was crashing down.

00:04:30.704 --> 00:04:33.575
Like all of these
abstractions we built upon,

00:04:33.575 --> 00:04:36.373
abstraction upon abstraction
upon abstraction,

00:04:36.373 --> 00:04:37.853
in the financial world,

00:04:37.853 --> 00:04:40.253
which no single human understood,

00:04:40.253 --> 00:04:41.991
were now falling apart.

00:04:41.991 --> 00:04:43.873
And suddenly we're getting back to basics,

00:04:43.873 --> 00:04:45.443
back to fundamentals.

00:04:45.443 --> 00:04:47.624
Management teams were
being asked to leave,

00:04:47.624 --> 00:04:49.743
banks were failing, you know,

00:04:49.743 --> 00:04:52.262
people were clarifying around, like,

00:04:52.262 --> 00:04:53.913
we need to start getting innovative,

00:04:53.913 --> 00:04:56.563
we need to stop treating
our customers so poorly,

00:04:56.563 --> 00:04:58.730
we need to stop, you know,

00:04:59.713 --> 00:05:02.101
encouraging bad behavior to reap fees.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:04.501
Like, we need to really turn
this whole thing around.

00:05:04.501 --> 00:05:07.253
And anytime there's a
recession or a depression,

00:05:07.253 --> 00:05:09.380
that's a great time to innovate,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.112
start a company, start a project,

00:05:12.112 --> 00:05:14.279
you know, do anything, so.

00:05:15.388 --> 00:05:17.728
You know, we recognize that time,

00:05:17.728 --> 00:05:21.324
and at the same time, my co-founder, Jim

00:05:21.324 --> 00:05:22.657
re-contacted me.

00:05:24.078 --> 00:05:25.768
I've known him since he was 15 years old,

00:05:25.768 --> 00:05:28.507
he was my boss when I was 15 years old.

00:05:28.507 --> 00:05:31.538
And he's like, "Jack,
I want to talk to you.

00:05:31.538 --> 00:05:34.098
"I want to build an
electronic car company."

00:05:34.098 --> 00:05:36.156
I'm like, that's interesting.

00:05:36.156 --> 00:05:38.038
And he was thinking about it because

00:05:38.038 --> 00:05:39.618
the car industry was failing as well,

00:05:39.618 --> 00:05:42.408
and he was like, why don't we
just give these car companies

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:44.489
to well-seasoned entrepreneurs?

00:05:44.489 --> 00:05:48.048
Like why don't we give Ford to Steve Jobs?

00:05:48.048 --> 00:05:48.938
- That would be amazing.

00:05:48.938 --> 00:05:51.051
- Yeah, it would be amazing.

00:05:51.051 --> 00:05:54.517
And like, you know, GM
to Ellison or whatnot.

00:05:54.517 --> 00:05:55.980
And he's like, "Also I want to start

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:56.828
"an electronic car company."

00:05:56.828 --> 00:05:57.909
I'm like...

00:05:57.909 --> 00:05:59.630
That's ambitious.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:00.998
But, in that moment, we decided

00:06:00.998 --> 00:06:02.558
not to start an electronic car company,

00:06:02.558 --> 00:06:04.859
but we wanted to do something together.

00:06:04.859 --> 00:06:07.000
And we kept having these
weekly conversations

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.489
about what that thing was.

00:06:08.489 --> 00:06:11.560
And I was researching a lot
with finance and healthcare,

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.289
'cause I was looking at, you know,

00:06:13.289 --> 00:06:15.094
doing something in those spaces.

00:06:15.094 --> 00:06:17.685
And Jim called me one day,

00:06:17.685 --> 00:06:19.317
one of our scheduled meetings,

00:06:19.317 --> 00:06:22.650
and he said, "I just lost a glass sale."

00:06:23.893 --> 00:06:26.204
He's an artist, he sells glass.

00:06:26.204 --> 00:06:29.662
"And I lost a sale because I
couldn't accept a credit card."

00:06:29.662 --> 00:06:32.218
And here he was, talking on his iPhone,

00:06:32.218 --> 00:06:33.426
his general purpose computer,

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.034
and I was talking on my iPhone.

00:06:35.034 --> 00:06:35.867
And like we couldn't figure out--

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:37.939
- A 1 GHz computer sitting
in the palm of your hand

00:06:37.939 --> 00:06:39.184
but no way to accept credit cards?

00:06:39.184 --> 00:06:41.531
- Yeah, he couldn't
accept $2,000 from someone

00:06:41.531 --> 00:06:42.655
who wanted to pay him.

00:06:42.655 --> 00:06:43.488
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:06:43.488 --> 00:06:44.321
- Like, why is that?

00:06:44.321 --> 00:06:47.061
So we decided that we wanted
to answer that question,

00:06:47.061 --> 00:06:48.383
and we did a bunch of research,

00:06:48.383 --> 00:06:50.673
and I started writing some server code,

00:06:50.673 --> 00:06:53.353
and we hired a guy to
write some iPhone code,

00:06:53.353 --> 00:06:55.203
and Jim built some hardware,

00:06:55.203 --> 00:06:58.123
and in a month, we had a little,

00:06:58.123 --> 00:07:00.423
well it was actually quite a big device

00:07:00.423 --> 00:07:03.135
that plugged into the
audio jacket of your iPhone

00:07:03.135 --> 00:07:05.991
and could swipe the card, take a payment.

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:07.828
It would go through the merchant process

00:07:07.828 --> 00:07:10.774
and then generate a
receipt with a map on it.

00:07:10.774 --> 00:07:14.649
And I went around, I showed
it to a bunch of investors,

00:07:14.649 --> 00:07:19.526
and they were all amazed
that it could even work.

00:07:19.526 --> 00:07:22.436
So we decided to really focus on it

00:07:22.436 --> 00:07:24.045
and make it a company.

00:07:24.045 --> 00:07:25.954
And it really just comes down to

00:07:25.954 --> 00:07:27.586
recognizing that fortunate situation,

00:07:27.586 --> 00:07:29.726
recognizing the people
around you that could help

00:07:29.726 --> 00:07:31.956
and just owning it.

00:07:31.956 --> 00:07:34.530
The biggest thing for me on that path is

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:37.119
I need to, I need to draw something out,

00:07:37.119 --> 00:07:38.460
and I need to get it out of my head.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.440
I found myself, very early on,

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.323
thinking about something, like,

00:07:42.323 --> 00:07:45.051
you know, thinking about
this early idea for Twitter,

00:07:45.051 --> 00:07:48.120
and saying to myself, "I
could build this awesome..."

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.337
You know, you have those,
like, shower moments

00:07:50.337 --> 00:07:51.615
or you're walking at midnight

00:07:51.615 --> 00:07:53.054
in some town in New York City,

00:07:53.054 --> 00:07:55.179
and you've got these amazing grand ideas.

00:07:55.179 --> 00:07:58.521
And then you start thinking, well,

00:07:58.521 --> 00:08:00.960
I could really start
doing this, if only X,

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.090
and if I had this person,

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:04.919
or if this technology existed,

00:08:04.919 --> 00:08:06.828
or if this happened, or this happened.

00:08:06.828 --> 00:08:09.726
And what I realized I was
doing is I was constantly

00:08:09.726 --> 00:08:11.978
making excuses for not working on it,

00:08:11.978 --> 00:08:13.781
and then the window had passed,

00:08:13.781 --> 00:08:15.543
and then I couldn't do anything.

00:08:15.543 --> 00:08:18.834
So I think it's really,
really important to

00:08:18.834 --> 00:08:21.491
write it out, or to draw it out.

00:08:21.491 --> 00:08:22.658
Or to code it.

00:08:23.845 --> 00:08:25.490
But you need to get it out of your head.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.101
And the reason you have to
get it out of your head is

00:08:27.101 --> 00:08:29.136
you need to be able to see it on a surface

00:08:29.136 --> 00:08:31.644
that is not in your mind.

00:08:31.644 --> 00:08:34.785
And once you can see it, and
once you can step back from it,

00:08:34.785 --> 00:08:36.268
then you can also decide,

00:08:36.268 --> 00:08:39.513
"Eh, you know, this passes my filter."

00:08:39.513 --> 00:08:41.123
Passes my, you know, constraints,

00:08:41.123 --> 00:08:44.805
so maybe I can show it and
share with some other people.

00:08:44.805 --> 00:08:46.611
And then they'll be like, "You know what,

00:08:46.611 --> 00:08:48.410
"that's the stupidest idea ever."

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.611
And, or, you know, "That's
somewhat interesting,

00:08:51.611 --> 00:08:53.807
"but maybe this and this and this."

00:08:53.807 --> 00:08:55.613
So the sooner you can do that,

00:08:55.613 --> 00:08:57.429
then you have a lot of momentum around it

00:08:57.429 --> 00:09:00.270
and you can, you know,
really decide if you want to

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.409
commit to it and work on it more

00:09:02.409 --> 00:09:06.561
or put it on the shelf for a later date.

00:09:06.561 --> 00:09:09.277
And the realization that I
think everyone needs to have

00:09:09.277 --> 00:09:12.611
about that latter option,
putting it on the shelf,

00:09:12.611 --> 00:09:14.177
is that you can come back to it.

00:09:14.177 --> 00:09:17.535
And it'll surface back up
in another piece of work,

00:09:17.535 --> 00:09:20.766
or another idea at some
point in your life.

00:09:20.766 --> 00:09:24.599
So, having that ability
to close off a chapter

00:09:25.525 --> 00:09:28.543
and move on is really, really important.

00:09:28.543 --> 00:09:30.151
You can't have all these open threads.

00:09:30.151 --> 00:09:32.414
And that's what I realized I was doing.

00:09:32.414 --> 00:09:36.750
And that also encouraged
me to really write more,

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.086
and to really think about, you know,

00:09:39.086 --> 00:09:40.586
what is the story?

00:09:41.452 --> 00:09:42.914
How are people coming to this?

00:09:42.914 --> 00:09:45.835
And when I show my friends this,

00:09:45.835 --> 00:09:47.260
how are they going to react?

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:48.093
And I would write it down.

00:09:48.093 --> 00:09:51.329
I would actually treat it like a play.

00:09:51.329 --> 00:09:53.976
And when I realized that
I was writing plays,

00:09:53.976 --> 00:09:57.251
I read a lot more plays, for style,

00:09:57.251 --> 00:09:59.116
and for substance, and for technique.

00:09:59.116 --> 00:10:03.079
And I think it's, you know,
I think it's really good.

00:10:03.079 --> 00:10:03.995
I think there's another company

00:10:03.995 --> 00:10:07.997
that I've always looked
towards for inspiration,

00:10:07.997 --> 00:10:11.055
and I know a number of people in this room

00:10:11.055 --> 00:10:13.725
probably have the similar company in mind,

00:10:13.725 --> 00:10:15.615
which is Apple.

00:10:15.615 --> 00:10:19.210
Apple, I think, is run
like a theater company.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.063
It has a great sense of pacing,

00:10:21.063 --> 00:10:24.016
it has a great sense of story,

00:10:24.016 --> 00:10:25.835
and has a great sense of execution.

00:10:25.835 --> 00:10:27.602
And it's all about...

00:10:27.602 --> 00:10:28.674
It's all event-driven.

00:10:28.674 --> 00:10:30.147
It's all stage-driven.

00:10:30.147 --> 00:10:31.216
The stage being a billboard,

00:10:31.216 --> 00:10:32.679
or the stage being a keynote,

00:10:32.679 --> 00:10:35.351
or the stage being a product launch.

00:10:35.351 --> 00:10:37.542
All of it has a very, very cohesive

00:10:37.542 --> 00:10:39.220
end-to-end story.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.741
I mean, you think about what happened

00:10:41.741 --> 00:10:44.040
when Steve Jobs came back to the company,

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.120
the first thing he did is he killed

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.812
every product line the
company was working on.

00:10:49.812 --> 00:10:51.838
And for two years, they had no product

00:10:51.838 --> 00:10:53.767
on the market whatsoever.

00:10:53.767 --> 00:10:56.023
All they had were a bunch of posters

00:10:56.023 --> 00:10:58.186
all around the world with Steve's heroes.

00:10:58.186 --> 00:10:59.909
And it said, "Think different."

00:10:59.909 --> 00:11:01.808
And it was just focused
on bringing up the brand

00:11:01.808 --> 00:11:04.234
and making people aware
of the brand again,

00:11:04.234 --> 00:11:05.812
and how the brand is aligning

00:11:05.812 --> 00:11:08.895
to this particular feeling and story.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.769
And then they came out with the iMac,

00:11:11.769 --> 00:11:15.287
and then built iTunes, and then the iPod,

00:11:15.287 --> 00:11:17.702
and they realized that, wait a minute,

00:11:17.702 --> 00:11:19.691
people are carrying music
on their phones now,

00:11:19.691 --> 00:11:22.901
so we better build a phone, the iPhone.

00:11:22.901 --> 00:11:27.434
So this unfolding of the
plot and the epic story

00:11:27.434 --> 00:11:29.503
has been very very interesting to watch,

00:11:29.503 --> 00:11:31.342
especially if you look back, you know,

00:11:31.342 --> 00:11:33.976
to that time when he
came back to the company.

00:11:33.976 --> 00:11:36.989
So I've learned a lot from that company,

00:11:36.989 --> 00:11:39.622
and other companies who
operate in a similar fashion.

00:11:39.622 --> 00:11:41.851
Constraint is a big thing for me,

00:11:41.851 --> 00:11:43.429
and this is something that
we're constantly learning

00:11:43.429 --> 00:11:45.179
not to fear, as well.

00:11:46.237 --> 00:11:48.403
It's important to say no.

00:11:48.403 --> 00:11:51.093
And it's important to be
able to easily say no.

00:11:51.093 --> 00:11:53.577
And there's many, many things you can do.

00:11:53.577 --> 00:11:57.327
Especially when you have
a successful venture

00:11:58.177 --> 00:12:00.995
behind you, or you're running with it.

00:12:00.995 --> 00:12:04.065
And it's really important
to recognize, you know,

00:12:04.065 --> 00:12:07.309
when, where you need to
go, where you'd like to go,

00:12:07.309 --> 00:12:09.184
and really constrain the process,

00:12:09.184 --> 00:12:12.382
and then keep things really simple.

00:12:12.382 --> 00:12:14.612
In my dispatch days,

00:12:14.612 --> 00:12:16.695
back in 1999, or in 2000,

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.671
we built something really cool.

00:12:19.671 --> 00:12:21.120
We loved it.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:24.510
And then we hired a CEO
that didn't really match

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:28.617
our personalities and
didn't match our vision,

00:12:28.617 --> 00:12:32.122
and we grew the company
very, very quickly.

00:12:32.122 --> 00:12:36.289
And that really scattered
the focus of the company,

00:12:37.138 --> 00:12:40.345
so one of the things I've
learned from Twitter is

00:12:40.345 --> 00:12:42.346
that, you know, that singular focus,

00:12:42.346 --> 00:12:43.338
that do one thing

00:12:43.338 --> 00:12:44.171
- Yeah.

00:12:44.171 --> 00:12:45.606
- And do it very well.

00:12:45.606 --> 00:12:46.439
That works out.

00:12:46.439 --> 00:12:48.793
I think the biggest thing for me,

00:12:48.793 --> 00:12:50.251
I think for all startups,

00:12:50.251 --> 00:12:53.168
is that it's really, really easy to

00:12:54.827 --> 00:12:57.077
consider any hire as, like,

00:12:58.462 --> 00:13:01.333
the length of the entire company.

00:13:01.333 --> 00:13:03.659
I think leaders of companies

00:13:03.659 --> 00:13:06.121
have to consider themselves editors.

00:13:06.121 --> 00:13:08.790
And you constantly have to edit the team.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:10.414
You constantly have to make sure

00:13:10.414 --> 00:13:11.929
that you have the best people in,

00:13:11.929 --> 00:13:13.459
and if there's any negative attribute

00:13:13.459 --> 00:13:16.437
within the team, person
or element or otherwise,

00:13:16.437 --> 00:13:17.549
you have to part ways.

00:13:17.549 --> 00:13:18.449
- [Interviewer] And
deal with it right away.

00:13:18.449 --> 00:13:19.600
- Yeah, right away.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:21.339
I mean, you have to say, like,

00:13:21.339 --> 00:13:23.098
"This is the best thing for the company,

00:13:23.098 --> 00:13:25.121
"and to be fair to both us, like,

00:13:25.121 --> 00:13:27.421
it's just not the right
time for both of us."

00:13:27.421 --> 00:13:29.328
Like any other relationship,

00:13:29.328 --> 00:13:31.053
you need to be able to
recognize what's good

00:13:31.053 --> 00:13:34.931
and what isn't optimal
and take action on it.

00:13:34.931 --> 00:13:38.479
So that's one thing that, you know,

00:13:38.479 --> 00:13:42.125
like with Twitter, we
grew very very slowly,

00:13:42.125 --> 00:13:46.544
and we didn't edit the
team in the best way.

00:13:46.544 --> 00:13:49.882
We didn't get out of the way
of ourselves often enough.

00:13:49.882 --> 00:13:51.730
That's something I would never do again.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:53.212
What I want to talk to you about today

00:13:53.212 --> 00:13:56.193
is a note which I call "Daily."

00:13:56.193 --> 00:13:59.328
So if you create a new
note, in your notepad,

00:13:59.328 --> 00:14:01.704
or something that you
check on a daily basis,

00:14:01.704 --> 00:14:04.876
this is the most important thing.

00:14:04.876 --> 00:14:06.209
Name it "Daily."

00:14:07.869 --> 00:14:10.286
And then write the word "Do:"

00:14:12.467 --> 00:14:16.634
And then go down a few lines
and write the word "Don't:"

00:14:18.761 --> 00:14:20.072
The one thing I would ask you to do,

00:14:20.072 --> 00:14:23.523
walking out of this, is
to, every single day,

00:14:23.523 --> 00:14:26.690
just for a week, wake up to this note,

00:14:27.859 --> 00:14:29.562
check it throughout the day,

00:14:29.562 --> 00:14:31.551
and then also before you fall asleep,

00:14:31.551 --> 00:14:32.840
check to make sure that you did

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:33.827
and you didn't do everything.

00:14:33.827 --> 00:14:36.023
So what you put in each one of these

00:14:36.023 --> 00:14:37.528
is you have a list of everything

00:14:37.528 --> 00:14:39.828
that you want to do every single day.

00:14:39.828 --> 00:14:41.738
And you have a list of things
that you don't want to do.

00:14:41.738 --> 00:14:44.188
And the easiest way to add to the don'ts

00:14:44.188 --> 00:14:46.578
is to notice something that
you never want to do it again.

00:14:46.578 --> 00:14:48.223
You never want to do again,

00:14:48.223 --> 00:14:49.972
and you just add that to it.

00:14:49.972 --> 00:14:51.271
Very, very simple.

00:14:51.271 --> 00:14:53.490
I'm just going to share some of mine;

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.423
these might be a little bit
personal and embarrassing,

00:14:55.423 --> 00:14:56.756
but here we are.

00:14:59.352 --> 00:15:01.411
Number one on my "do" list

00:15:01.411 --> 00:15:02.994
is to stay present.

00:15:03.872 --> 00:15:06.875
I think it's so easy to
get trapped in the past.

00:15:06.875 --> 00:15:09.198
It's so easy to think about the future.

00:15:09.198 --> 00:15:11.268
The most important
thing is I stay present,

00:15:11.268 --> 00:15:13.858
and just reading this brings
me back to the present,

00:15:13.858 --> 00:15:16.722
brings me back to this moment.

00:15:16.722 --> 00:15:20.193
Number two for me is be vulnerable.

00:15:20.193 --> 00:15:22.884
Show people my mistakes,

00:15:22.884 --> 00:15:25.013
show people my fears,

00:15:25.013 --> 00:15:26.899
because they can relate.

00:15:26.899 --> 00:15:30.210
Because they're going
through similar things.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:35.017
Number three: drink only
lemon water and red wine.

00:15:35.017 --> 00:15:37.600
(crowd laughs)

00:15:40.364 --> 00:15:44.019
Great red wine is a great modifier for it.

00:15:44.019 --> 00:15:45.480
This goes with one of my "don'ts",

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:49.599
which is "don't drink hard
liquor or beer on weekdays."

00:15:49.599 --> 00:15:52.432
(audience laughs)

00:15:54.266 --> 00:15:58.433
Number three is six sets
of 20 squats and push-ups.

00:15:59.824 --> 00:16:00.708
Throughout the day.

00:16:00.708 --> 00:16:03.078
I just have to do them every day.

00:16:03.078 --> 00:16:06.040
Six sets of 30 second planks.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:07.707
Run for three miles.

00:16:08.767 --> 00:16:11.426
Meditate on this list.

00:16:11.426 --> 00:16:13.646
Stand up straight.

00:16:13.646 --> 00:16:14.479
Which, whenever I say,

00:16:14.479 --> 00:16:18.315
I always see posture
moving in the audience.

00:16:18.315 --> 00:16:19.922
Say hello to everyone.

00:16:19.922 --> 00:16:20.936
I just bought a heavy bag,

00:16:20.936 --> 00:16:22.154
so I'm training with a heavy bag,

00:16:22.154 --> 00:16:25.455
so I want to spend ten minutes
with the heavy bag every day.

00:16:25.455 --> 00:16:28.583
I'm going to do a video
journal every single day.

00:16:28.583 --> 00:16:31.666
And I want to get seven hours of sleep.

00:16:32.551 --> 00:16:33.840
On my "don't" list; and again,

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:35.415
this is one of the hardest things,

00:16:35.415 --> 00:16:36.626
and a lot of these are personal,

00:16:36.626 --> 00:16:39.627
so I'm not going to read all of them.

00:16:39.627 --> 00:16:43.361
But on my "don't" list is
don't avoid eye contact.

00:16:43.361 --> 00:16:44.528
Don't be late.

00:16:46.181 --> 00:16:50.066
Don't set expectations for
someone and not meet them.

00:16:50.066 --> 00:16:51.399
Don't eat sugar.

00:16:52.857 --> 00:16:54.746
I'm on the paleo diet, so don't eat

00:16:54.746 --> 00:16:56.829
wheat, lentils, or dairy.

00:16:58.381 --> 00:17:00.759
And then, of course, don't
drink hard liquor or beer.

00:17:00.759 --> 00:17:02.693
On the weekdays.

00:17:02.693 --> 00:17:05.485
So this list, while it
sounds very, very simple,

00:17:05.485 --> 00:17:09.606
has been fundamental in
establishing patterns for myself.

00:17:09.606 --> 00:17:12.513
And it's something that I do
check every single morning.

00:17:12.513 --> 00:17:14.379
I check throughout the day,

00:17:14.379 --> 00:17:16.469
and I check right before I go to bed.

00:17:16.469 --> 00:17:18.829
And I make sure that
I check everything off

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:19.807
and go through it.

00:17:19.807 --> 00:17:22.544
I've given it to our companies.

00:17:22.544 --> 00:17:25.017
I've given it to a number of
students that I've talked to

00:17:25.017 --> 00:17:27.762
as we've gone to schools and recruiting.

00:17:27.762 --> 00:17:29.396
And people have come back to me and said,

00:17:29.396 --> 00:17:31.489
"This is something that works for me."

00:17:31.489 --> 00:17:32.791
It's something that's easy to do,

00:17:32.791 --> 00:17:35.758
and something easy to remember.

00:17:35.758 --> 00:17:37.851
One of the most
fundamental things about it

00:17:37.851 --> 00:17:39.460
that I've learned, that
I've taken away from it,

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:41.856
is how it gives you focus.

00:17:41.856 --> 00:17:45.121
How it gives you something that
allows you to really ignore

00:17:45.121 --> 00:17:46.763
everything else that's going on.

00:17:46.763 --> 00:17:48.419
All the other noise.

00:17:48.419 --> 00:17:50.468
To allow you to focus on
what's most important.

00:17:50.468 --> 00:17:53.524
So this "do and don't" list, for people,

00:17:53.524 --> 00:17:55.411
and individuals in the company,

00:17:55.411 --> 00:17:57.836
has translated to a do and don't list

00:17:57.836 --> 00:17:59.610
for our company.

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:01.853
For Square, we have a do and don't list.

00:18:01.853 --> 00:18:03.920
We have "these are the things
that we're going to do,"

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:06.566
"these are our core things,"
"these are our bold bets,"

00:18:06.566 --> 00:18:09.809
and "these are the things we're
saying no to for right now."

00:18:09.809 --> 00:18:12.720
It's been fundamental in
allowing us to move fast,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:14.756
to continue to innovate,

00:18:14.756 --> 00:18:16.481
and to really push the boundaries

00:18:16.481 --> 00:18:20.388
and continue and invent
and question and reset

00:18:20.388 --> 00:18:22.393
everything that we think
about the organization.

00:18:22.393 --> 00:18:25.726
And one of my greatest
lessons that I learned

00:18:25.726 --> 00:18:27.406
in starting and running Twitter

00:18:27.406 --> 00:18:29.776
and starting and running Square,

00:18:29.776 --> 00:18:31.699
which is how important it is

00:18:31.699 --> 00:18:33.699
to instrument all usage.

00:18:35.058 --> 00:18:36.530
You have to instrument everything.

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:39.771
For the first two years of Twitter's life,

00:18:39.771 --> 00:18:41.243
we were flying blind.

00:18:41.243 --> 00:18:43.500
We had no idea what was
going with the network,

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:46.223
we had no idea what was
going on with the system,

00:18:46.223 --> 00:18:48.072
with how people were using it.

00:18:48.072 --> 00:18:49.112
We were making guesses,

00:18:49.112 --> 00:18:50.914
we were basing everything on intuition

00:18:50.914 --> 00:18:52.457
instead of having a good balance

00:18:52.457 --> 00:18:54.707
between intuition and data.

00:18:55.597 --> 00:18:57.574
And we were going down all
the time because of it.

00:18:57.574 --> 00:19:00.207
Because we could not
see what was happening.

00:19:00.207 --> 00:19:02.083
So the first thing I wrote for Square,

00:19:02.083 --> 00:19:04.910
on the server, was an admin dashboard.

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:06.993
And we have very, very strong discipline

00:19:06.993 --> 00:19:09.902
within the company,
which is now 72 people,

00:19:09.902 --> 00:19:13.135
to log everything, to measure everything,

00:19:13.135 --> 00:19:15.042
and to test everything.

00:19:15.042 --> 00:19:17.471
And we treat the dashboard,
we treat the analytics,

00:19:17.471 --> 00:19:20.598
we treat the data, as a product.

00:19:20.598 --> 00:19:23.369
And we call it the Inference Team,

00:19:23.369 --> 00:19:26.000
and their job is to instrument all usage,

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.750
and infer all action.

00:19:29.095 --> 00:19:31.052
And that's something that we need,

00:19:31.052 --> 00:19:34.190
but it's also something
that our users need.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:36.917
"Art, when really understood,

00:19:36.917 --> 00:19:39.514
"is a province of every human being.

00:19:39.514 --> 00:19:43.681
"It is simply a question of
doing things, anything, well.

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:48.261
"It is not an outside, extra thing.

00:19:48.261 --> 00:19:51.031
"When the artist is alive in any person,

00:19:51.031 --> 00:19:53.368
"whatever his kind of work may be,

00:19:53.368 --> 00:19:56.607
"he becomes an inventive,
searching, daring,

00:19:56.607 --> 00:19:58.955
"self-expressing creature.

00:19:58.955 --> 00:20:01.395
"He becomes interesting to other people.

00:20:01.395 --> 00:20:04.145
"He disturbs, upsets, enlightens,

00:20:05.919 --> 00:20:09.260
"and he opens ways for
better understanding.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:10.733
"Where those who are not artists

00:20:10.733 --> 00:20:14.259
"are trying to close
the book, he opens it.

00:20:14.259 --> 00:20:17.234
"He shows there are still
more pages possible."

00:20:17.234 --> 00:20:19.632
And I think that's so
telling for everything

00:20:19.632 --> 00:20:21.058
that you all are about to do,

00:20:21.058 --> 00:20:23.121
all the challenges you're about to face.

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:26.185
You're going to be the
ones that open the book.

00:20:26.185 --> 00:20:28.219
"The world would stagnate without him,

00:20:28.219 --> 00:20:30.459
"and the world would
be beautiful with him;

00:20:30.459 --> 00:20:32.657
"for he is interesting to himself

00:20:32.657 --> 00:20:35.032
"and he is interesting to others.

00:20:35.032 --> 00:20:37.047
"He does not have to be
a painter or a sculptor

00:20:37.047 --> 00:20:38.509
"to be an artist.

00:20:38.509 --> 00:20:40.831
"He can work in any medium.

00:20:40.831 --> 00:20:44.408
"He simply has to find the
gain in the work itself,

00:20:44.408 --> 00:20:45.906
"not outside of it."

00:20:45.906 --> 00:20:47.619
And one of the biggest
lessons that I've learned

00:20:47.619 --> 00:20:50.445
throughout my career is
how important the work is.

00:20:50.445 --> 00:20:52.343
How important not just the end product is,

00:20:52.343 --> 00:20:53.844
but the actual craft.

00:20:53.844 --> 00:20:54.677
Doing the work.

00:20:54.677 --> 00:20:56.927
Inventing within the work.

00:20:57.983 --> 00:21:00.969
"The work of the art
student is no light matter.

00:21:00.969 --> 00:21:04.816
"Few have the courage and
stamina to see it through.

00:21:04.816 --> 00:21:08.007
"You have to make up your
mind to be alone in many ways.

00:21:08.007 --> 00:21:11.508
"We like sympathy, as humans,
and we like to be in company.

00:21:11.508 --> 00:21:13.859
"It is easier than going it alone.

00:21:13.859 --> 00:21:17.632
"But alone one gets
acquainted with himself,

00:21:17.632 --> 00:21:21.946
"grows up and on, not
stopping with the crowd.

00:21:21.946 --> 00:21:23.933
"It costs to do this.

00:21:23.933 --> 00:21:27.957
"If you succeed somewhat
you may have to pay for it

00:21:27.957 --> 00:21:32.109
"as well as enjoy it for
the rest of your life."

00:21:32.109 --> 00:21:34.108
And that's something, if
you do something meaningful,

00:21:34.108 --> 00:21:37.221
you are going to have to
pay for it in all the work,

00:21:37.221 --> 00:21:39.156
but at the same time you
will also be able to enjoy it

00:21:39.156 --> 00:21:41.596
for the rest of your life.

00:21:41.596 --> 00:21:45.758
"We are not here to do what
has already been done."

00:21:45.758 --> 00:21:47.321
Everyone in this room feels that;

00:21:47.321 --> 00:21:51.532
we are not here to do what
has already been done.

00:21:51.532 --> 00:21:53.921
"Know what the old masters did.

00:21:53.921 --> 00:21:56.294
"Know how they composed their pictures,

00:21:56.294 --> 00:22:00.934
"but do not fall into the
conventions they established.

00:22:00.934 --> 00:22:03.659
"These conventions were right for them,

00:22:03.659 --> 00:22:05.346
"and they are wonderful.

00:22:05.346 --> 00:22:07.120
"They made their language.

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:08.920
"You make yours.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:10.621
"They can help you.

00:22:10.621 --> 00:22:13.018
"All the past can help you."

00:22:13.018 --> 00:22:15.643
And I think in Silicon Valley,
especially in technology,

00:22:15.643 --> 00:22:19.043
it's so easy to fall in
the footsteps of others.

00:22:19.043 --> 00:22:22.606
To do what they do because
you think it's the right way

00:22:22.606 --> 00:22:24.869
because you think they've had the success

00:22:24.869 --> 00:22:26.593
and you can copy that success.

00:22:26.593 --> 00:22:28.280
You have to find your own path.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:30.942
You have to find your own footsteps.

00:22:30.942 --> 00:22:34.481
"An art student must be a
master from the beginning;

00:22:34.481 --> 00:22:38.314
"that is, he must be
master of such as he has.

00:22:39.269 --> 00:22:42.682
"By being now master of such
as he has there is promise

00:22:42.682 --> 00:22:45.294
"that he will be a master in the future."

00:22:45.294 --> 00:22:48.233
What he means by this is purely,

00:22:48.233 --> 00:22:50.881
you have to be a master of your own tools.

00:22:50.881 --> 00:22:54.219
That mastership is not a destination,

00:22:54.219 --> 00:22:56.921
it is a process, and
it's constant practice

00:22:56.921 --> 00:22:58.432
that gets you there.

00:22:58.432 --> 00:23:01.733
"It is not enough to
have thought great things

00:23:01.733 --> 00:23:04.381
"before doing the work."

00:23:04.381 --> 00:23:05.495
We have so many ideas,

00:23:05.495 --> 00:23:07.458
but what really matters is the work

00:23:07.458 --> 00:23:09.541
to implement those ideas.

00:23:13.358 --> 00:23:15.571
"Don't worry about the rejections.

00:23:15.571 --> 00:23:19.519
"Everybody that's good
has gone through it.

00:23:19.519 --> 00:23:21.434
"Don't let it matter if your works

00:23:21.434 --> 00:23:24.184
"are not 'accepted' at once.

00:23:24.184 --> 00:23:26.031
"The better or more personal you are

00:23:26.031 --> 00:23:28.821
"the less likely they are of acceptance.

00:23:28.821 --> 00:23:32.082
"Just remember that the
object of painting pictures

00:23:32.082 --> 00:23:35.331
"is not simply to get them in exhibition.

00:23:35.331 --> 00:23:38.142
"It is all very fine to
have your pictures hung,

00:23:38.142 --> 00:23:42.172
"but you are painting for
yourself, not for the jury."

00:23:42.172 --> 00:23:44.843
And what's meaningful
about this passage to me is

00:23:44.843 --> 00:23:48.982
we work so hard to get some
sort of acceptance in the world.

00:23:48.982 --> 00:23:51.184
To get some sort of positive feedback.

00:23:51.184 --> 00:23:54.756
And we look at others, and it
seems so fast, their success.

00:23:54.756 --> 00:23:56.746
It seems like they did
it in just a moment.

00:23:56.746 --> 00:24:00.913
But it takes years, and years,
and years, and patience.

00:24:03.821 --> 00:24:07.321
The marking interface is not easy on this.

00:24:08.182 --> 00:24:10.783
"There's a time and place for all things;

00:24:10.783 --> 00:24:13.069
"the difficulty is to use them only

00:24:13.069 --> 00:24:16.195
"in their proper time and place."

00:24:16.195 --> 00:24:17.659
There's a time and place for all things.

00:24:17.659 --> 00:24:19.618
The difficulty is to use them only

00:24:19.618 --> 00:24:22.201
in their proper time and place.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:27.146
"With motive, you will
become clairvoyant of means,

00:24:27.146 --> 00:24:29.405
"you will seize and command them.

00:24:29.405 --> 00:24:32.944
"Without motive, you will wobble about."

00:24:32.944 --> 00:24:36.032
This, in building a team,
in building an organization,

00:24:36.032 --> 00:24:38.582
in building a company, is
one of the greatest lessons.

00:24:38.582 --> 00:24:40.033
You cannot do anything without

00:24:40.033 --> 00:24:42.682
a shared and common sense of purpose.

00:24:42.682 --> 00:24:44.945
If you don't have motive,
if you don't have purpose

00:24:44.945 --> 00:24:46.667
that is shared between everyone,

00:24:46.667 --> 00:24:48.269
you will wander about.

00:24:48.269 --> 00:24:50.178
You will wobble.

00:24:50.178 --> 00:24:52.994
And you will not do
anything of infinite means,

00:24:52.994 --> 00:24:56.494
you will not do anything that is timeless.

00:25:01.632 --> 00:25:02.869
And lastly:

00:25:02.869 --> 00:25:06.446
"The art student of
these days is a pioneer."

00:25:06.446 --> 00:25:08.358
Just like all of you.

00:25:08.358 --> 00:25:10.259
"The art student of today must pioneer

00:25:10.259 --> 00:25:13.009
"beyond the mere matters of fact.

00:25:14.246 --> 00:25:16.347
"I believe the great artists of the future

00:25:16.347 --> 00:25:20.570
"will use fewer words, copy fewer things.

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:22.343
"Essays will be shorter in words,

00:25:22.343 --> 00:25:24.795
"and longer in meaning."

00:25:24.795 --> 00:25:25.671
I love this passage.

00:25:25.671 --> 00:25:27.246
"I believe the great artists of the future

00:25:27.246 --> 00:25:31.244
"will use fewer words, copy fewer things.

00:25:31.244 --> 00:25:35.411
"Essays will be shorter in
words and longer in meaning.

00:25:36.506 --> 00:25:40.181
"We must paint only
what is important to us.

00:25:40.181 --> 00:25:43.018
"We must not respond to outside demands.

00:25:43.018 --> 00:25:45.158
"They do not know what they want

00:25:45.158 --> 00:25:47.396
"or what we have to give."

00:25:47.396 --> 00:25:48.768
I think one of the most important things

00:25:48.768 --> 00:25:50.803
about what all of you do

00:25:50.803 --> 00:25:54.042
is that you are building what
you want to see in the world.

00:25:54.042 --> 00:25:56.230
You're making the bet that other people

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:58.080
want to see the same thing in the world.

00:25:58.080 --> 00:25:59.669
Sometimes you lose the bet,

00:25:59.669 --> 00:26:01.592
sometimes you win the bet.

00:26:01.592 --> 00:26:03.192
But the most important thing is you have

00:26:03.192 --> 00:26:04.845
that passion to build for yourself,

00:26:04.845 --> 00:26:06.863
because that is what's infectious,

00:26:06.863 --> 00:26:09.265
and that is what brings other people

00:26:09.265 --> 00:26:11.848
to your cause and to your team.

00:26:14.124 --> 00:26:15.389
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:26:15.389 --> 00:26:16.888
I hope you enjoyed this video.

00:26:16.888 --> 00:26:19.576
I made it because Digvijay asked me to.

00:26:19.576 --> 00:26:20.711
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:26:20.711 --> 00:26:22.600
that you want me to profile next,

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:23.687
leave it in the comments below,

00:26:23.687 --> 00:26:24.789
and I'll see what I can do.

00:26:24.789 --> 00:26:25.622
I'd also love to know,

00:26:25.622 --> 00:26:27.935
what did Jack say that
really inspired you,

00:26:27.935 --> 00:26:29.648
that motivated you, that made you think

00:26:29.648 --> 00:26:30.925
or question something?

00:26:30.925 --> 00:26:31.763
Leave it in the comments,

00:26:31.763 --> 00:26:33.649
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:26:33.649 --> 00:26:35.438
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:26:35.438 --> 00:26:36.439
I believe in you,

00:26:36.439 --> 00:26:38.076
I hope you believe in yourself

00:26:38.076 --> 00:26:40.122
and whatever your one word is.

00:26:40.122 --> 00:26:41.955
And I'll see you soon.

00:26:47.415 --> 00:26:48.739
- [Man With Red Shirt] How do you go about

00:26:48.739 --> 00:26:51.116
attracting funding and then
managing your investors

00:26:51.116 --> 00:26:53.407
after you've secured it?

00:26:53.407 --> 00:26:56.593
- [Jack] I think the thing
that's always worked for me

00:26:56.593 --> 00:26:58.260
is realizing that...

00:27:01.654 --> 00:27:03.404
Your investors are...

00:27:04.527 --> 00:27:06.889
First and foremost, I
always want to go to anyone

00:27:06.889 --> 00:27:10.722
that I want to work with
with something to show.

00:27:11.639 --> 00:27:13.202
And it was really important to us,

00:27:13.202 --> 00:27:15.500
before we talked to any investors,

00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:17.250
on the Twitter side, that
we actually had people

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:19.917
using the service at some scale.

00:27:21.949 --> 00:27:23.298
And on the Square side, we actually

00:27:23.298 --> 00:27:25.160
had a working prototype.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.996
At Square we had, really, seven merchants

00:27:27.996 --> 00:27:29.800
when we actually went to raise money.

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:31.322
But we had a working prototype

00:27:31.322 --> 00:27:33.735
that people were actually using.

00:27:33.735 --> 00:27:35.454
If people can't see it and feel it,

00:27:35.454 --> 00:27:37.406
it's very, very hard to sell.

00:27:37.406 --> 00:27:39.484
I don't want to just go and tell people,

00:27:39.484 --> 00:27:41.196
"This is going to be
the biggest thing ever."

00:27:41.196 --> 00:27:42.880
I want to be able to show that,

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:46.021
and have them feel it and walk
away feeling that as well.

00:27:46.021 --> 00:27:47.716
So that's first and foremost,

00:27:47.716 --> 00:27:49.883
is have something to show.

00:27:51.366 --> 00:27:52.708
So that they can actually feel

00:27:52.708 --> 00:27:56.307
and start making it a part
of their story as well

00:27:56.307 --> 00:27:58.430
and feel like they have ownership over it.

00:27:58.430 --> 00:28:00.829
And then second is the realization, like,

00:28:00.829 --> 00:28:04.329
I always kind of approach this as, like...

00:28:05.635 --> 00:28:07.181
If we take money from someone,

00:28:07.181 --> 00:28:10.932
this is kind of like hiring
someone that we can never fire.

00:28:10.932 --> 00:28:12.706
We can never get rid of, right?

00:28:12.706 --> 00:28:16.919
So that means, do we really
want to work with this person?

00:28:16.919 --> 00:28:19.339
Is this person going to
be someone that really

00:28:19.339 --> 00:28:23.006
pushes us in the way that
we need to be pushed?

00:28:23.006 --> 00:28:25.481
And you can tell that fairly quickly.

00:28:25.481 --> 00:28:27.781
In that first week of pitching Square,

00:28:27.781 --> 00:28:30.198
got a lot of people who said,

00:28:31.567 --> 00:28:33.307
"Okay, great, we don't have any questions,

00:28:33.307 --> 00:28:35.331
"we'll get back to you
in about 30 minutes,

00:28:35.331 --> 00:28:37.679
"probably with a term sheet."

00:28:37.679 --> 00:28:39.580
And I said, "I don't want a term sheet.

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:42.056
Especially if you're not
asking any questions."

00:28:42.056 --> 00:28:44.532
'Cause they asked no
questions during the pitch.

00:28:44.532 --> 00:28:48.093
And that means if they're on our board

00:28:48.093 --> 00:28:50.005
that they're actually not going to be

00:28:50.005 --> 00:28:51.944
all that constructive in terms of really

00:28:51.944 --> 00:28:53.719
asking the tough questions and making sure

00:28:53.719 --> 00:28:56.980
that we're, you know, we're
not just in our own bubble,

00:28:56.980 --> 00:28:58.096
but they're looking outside.

00:28:58.096 --> 00:28:59.195
That is the role of the Board,

00:28:59.195 --> 00:29:01.833
is to look outside and bring all their

00:29:01.833 --> 00:29:03.172
insight and wisdom to us,

00:29:03.172 --> 00:29:06.339
so that we can help guide the company.

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:11.191
And that second week, we got
a lot of tough questions,

00:29:11.191 --> 00:29:13.475
and that's what I love,
because I know if I'm getting

00:29:13.475 --> 00:29:14.882
tough questions in the pitch,

00:29:14.882 --> 00:29:16.918
I'm going to get tough questions
in the boardroom as well.

00:29:16.918 --> 00:29:19.672
And not just the
boardroom, but every week.

00:29:19.672 --> 00:29:23.081
I mean, Vinod emails me once a week about,

00:29:23.081 --> 00:29:24.033
"Have you thought about this?

00:29:24.033 --> 00:29:25.096
"Are you doing this?"

00:29:25.096 --> 00:29:27.458
And, you know, "I know this person,

00:29:27.458 --> 00:29:29.080
"can I make an introduction here?"

00:29:29.080 --> 00:29:31.858
And it's just very, very useful.

00:29:31.858 --> 00:29:32.943
So I'm looking for someone

00:29:32.943 --> 00:29:35.468
who I just love working with.

00:29:35.468 --> 00:29:38.753
And I think you have to
set a very, very high bar,

00:29:38.753 --> 00:29:42.593
it's not just about money
or the firm, it's about the

00:29:42.593 --> 00:29:44.782
individual that you're
ultimately going to work with.

00:29:44.782 --> 00:29:47.782
And people ask me all the time, you know,

00:29:47.782 --> 00:29:50.619
"What firms would you suggest I go pitch?"

00:29:50.619 --> 00:29:53.682
I'm like, find the people that
you really want to work with

00:29:53.682 --> 00:29:57.796
in those firms and if
there's someone that just

00:29:57.796 --> 00:30:01.219
really resonates with
you and you love the idea

00:30:01.219 --> 00:30:03.394
of working with them, focus on them.

00:30:03.394 --> 00:30:07.084
And if it tests out to be the case, great.

00:30:07.084 --> 00:30:10.668
But I think you have to
kind of treat it as like,

00:30:10.668 --> 00:30:13.501
I'm adding this person to my team.

00:30:14.609 --> 00:30:16.306
One of the biggest things

00:30:16.306 --> 00:30:17.806
that has helped me

00:30:18.755 --> 00:30:22.672
is learning how to become
a better storyteller,

00:30:23.707 --> 00:30:26.279
and the power of a story.

00:30:26.279 --> 00:30:27.862
And by this I mean,

00:30:28.844 --> 00:30:30.903
if you want to build a product,

00:30:30.903 --> 00:30:31.736
and you want to build a product

00:30:31.736 --> 00:30:33.903
that is relevant to folks,

00:30:35.399 --> 00:30:37.898
you need to put yourself in their shoes.

00:30:37.898 --> 00:30:41.676
And you need to write a
story from their side.

00:30:41.676 --> 00:30:45.087
So we spend a lot of time writing

00:30:45.087 --> 00:30:47.472
what's called "user narratives."

00:30:47.472 --> 00:30:50.725
Of "this user," or "this person"

00:30:50.725 --> 00:30:52.249
"is in the middle of Chicago,

00:30:52.249 --> 00:30:54.210
and they go to a coffee store

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:55.559
in the middle of Chicago,

00:30:55.559 --> 00:30:58.197
and this is the experience
they're going to have."

00:30:58.197 --> 00:30:59.947
It reads like a play.

00:31:00.998 --> 00:31:02.721
It's really, really beautiful.

00:31:02.721 --> 00:31:05.308
And if you do that story well,

00:31:05.308 --> 00:31:09.308
then all the prioritization,
all of the product,

00:31:10.623 --> 00:31:13.257
all of the design, and
all the coordination

00:31:13.257 --> 00:31:15.960
that you need to do with these products

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:17.949
just falls out naturally.

00:31:17.949 --> 00:31:19.211
Because you can edit the story,

00:31:19.211 --> 00:31:21.286
and everyone can relate to the story

00:31:21.286 --> 00:31:23.184
from all levels of the organization;

00:31:23.184 --> 00:31:28.177
engineers, to operations,
to support, to designers,

00:31:28.177 --> 00:31:32.175
to, you know, the business
side of the house.

00:31:32.175 --> 00:31:34.938
So that story is very,
very important for us.

00:31:34.938 --> 00:31:37.353
And really constantly
considering the story

00:31:37.353 --> 00:31:39.540
and what new twists and elements

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:41.238
we need to add to the story

00:31:41.238 --> 00:31:43.277
are a pretty big deal for us, as well.

00:31:43.277 --> 00:31:46.778
And we want to tell an epic story.

00:31:46.778 --> 00:31:49.778
We want to solve a really big problem.

00:31:50.925 --> 00:31:51.852
We don't want to have a bunch

00:31:51.852 --> 00:31:53.965
of short stories strung together.

00:31:53.965 --> 00:31:55.989
We want one epic, cohesive story

00:31:55.989 --> 00:31:57.089
that we tell the world.

00:31:57.089 --> 00:31:59.839
And both Twitter and Square

00:31:59.839 --> 00:32:02.339
are driving towards this goal.

00:32:03.641 --> 00:32:06.515
So number two, good storytelling,

00:32:06.515 --> 00:32:09.428
and writing stories for the users.

00:32:09.428 --> 00:32:11.064
- [Interviewer] Who's your favorite CEO?

00:32:11.064 --> 00:32:13.643
- [Jack] Who's my favorite CEO...

00:32:13.643 --> 00:32:14.943
- [Interviewer] Who's your role model?

00:32:14.943 --> 00:32:17.142
- I always look up to

00:32:17.142 --> 00:32:18.193
Steve Jobs.
- Steve Jobs?

00:32:18.193 --> 00:32:19.978
(chuckling)

00:32:19.978 --> 00:32:23.652
- I know it's a common one, but
I think that's for a reason.

00:32:23.652 --> 00:32:26.292
I think he's done some amazing things.

00:32:26.292 --> 00:32:28.959
He has a great pacing about him.

00:32:30.831 --> 00:32:33.140
You know, there are a
lot of horror stories.

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:35.665
He is a very opinionated person.

00:32:35.665 --> 00:32:37.748
But he has the direction,

00:32:38.840 --> 00:32:41.862
and he knows what he wants to do.

00:32:41.862 --> 00:32:43.088
And he does it.

00:32:43.088 --> 00:32:43.921
- Yeah.

00:32:43.921 --> 00:32:47.049
- And he makes sure that when, you know,

00:32:47.049 --> 00:32:48.674
he's not so good in there,

00:32:48.674 --> 00:32:51.225
he recognizes it and he fills that area.

00:32:51.225 --> 00:32:53.725
And that's really important
for any CEO, I think,

00:32:53.725 --> 00:32:56.549
is to recognize your weaknesses

00:32:56.549 --> 00:32:58.632
and bring amazing people.

00:32:59.585 --> 00:33:01.534
And bring people in
that are much smarter--

00:33:01.534 --> 00:33:02.635
- [Interviewer] Smarter than you.

00:33:02.635 --> 00:33:05.859
- And you know, just create
a creative work environment.

